+/*
+ * bwfm(4): Broadcom/Cypress FullMAC WiFi, FreeBSD port of OpenBSD's driver.
+ *
+ * The chip, BCDC and firmware-variable code is OpenBSD's.  The net80211
+ * side is new, because FreeBSD's net80211 has vaps and no in-kernel
+ * supplicant:
+ *
+ * - ieee80211com "bwfmN" per device; vaps are cloned on it
+ *   (ifconfig wlan0 create wlandev bwfm0).  One vap for now.
+ * - Scanning is offloaded (IEEE80211_FEXT_SCAN_OFFLOAD): ic_scan_start
+ *   starts a firmware escan, every reported BSS is turned into a
+ *   synthetic beacon, parsed by ieee80211_parse_beacon() and entered with
+ *   ieee80211_add_scan(); the final escan event ends the scan.
+ * - Joining: entering AUTH issues the firmware join for vap->iv_bss.  The
+ *   firmware authenticates/associates on its own; its events move the vap
+ *   AUTH -> RUN, or back to SCAN on failure/link loss.
+ * - WPA/WPA2-PSK: when a PSK was set with IEEE80211_IOC_WPAPSK
+ *   (ifconfig wlan0 wpakey ...) the firmware supplicant runs the 4-way
+ *   handshake (sup_wpa=1 + WLC_SET_WSEC_PMK); the vap reaches RUN after
+ *   the firmware reports the handshake complete (E_PSK_SUP).  Without a
+ *   PSK but with a supplicant-provided WPA IE the host supplicant
+ *   (wpa_supplicant -D bsd) gets the EAPOL frames and installs keys with
+ *   the usual key ioctls.
+ * - Data: the firmware speaks 802.3.  The vap's if_transmit hands Ethernet
+ *   frames to the bus; received Ethernet frames go to the vap's if_input.
+ *   EAPOL frames take the same path in both directions, so a host-side
+ *   authenticator for a future hostap vap can see them.
+ */
 
